# Engineering Handbook — Quick Reference Cheat Sheets

Applies to: `kubernetes-handbook`, `postgresql-cheatsheet`, and similar **cheat-sheet** Engineering Handbooks (not Java — see `java-cheatsheet-posts.mdc`; not Spring Boot — see `spring-boot-handbook-posts.mdc`).

## Audience & Tone

Readers **already know the concepts**. Pages are **quick recap + copy-paste snippets** — not tutorials, not architecture deep dives, not "when to choose" decision guides.

| Section | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Engineering Handbooks** | Cheat sheet — commands, YAML, config, API snippets |
| **Microservices** | Architecture patterns — why/how in distributed systems |
| **Technology Playbook** | Decision guides — when to pick tool A vs B |

## Hugo Front Matter

```yaml
---
title: "<Topic>"
date: 2026-06-30T10:00:00+00:00
draft: false
description: "One-line recap for SEO."
tags: ["<section-tag>", "cheatsheet", "handbook"]
categories: ["<Section Title>"]
shortTitle: "<Short>"
module: 1
moduleTitle: "<Module Name>"
sectionRef: "1.1"
---
```

Use `categories: ["Kubernetes Handbook"]` or `["Spring Boot Handbook"]` — never `Technology Playbook`.

## Required Sections (use `##` headings)

Keep pages scannable. Prefer **tables and code blocks** over prose.

1. **Executive Summary** — one short paragraph
2. **Core Concepts** — Mermaid diagram (optional) + reference table
3. **Quick Reference** — grouped CLI commands, API calls, or config keys
4. **Snippets** — Dockerfile, kubectl YAML, `application.yml`, Java config — whatever fits the topic
5. **Common Gotchas** — bullets; use `note` / `warning` callouts sparingly
6. **Related Topics** — internal links (prev/next + section index + Microservices deep-dive if exists)

Separate major sections with `---`.

## Omit (belongs elsewhere)

- "When to Choose" / "When Not to Choose" decision matrices → **Technology Playbook**
- Namespace/cgroup architecture teardown → **Microservices**
- Long interview-answer blocks → **Interview Prep**
- Messaging-broker or unrelated stub templates

## Callouts

Reuse site shortcodes: `note`, `tip`, `warning` (same syntax as Java posts).

